<Legal
  heading="Terms of Use"
  kicker="The rules of the road"
  description="The terms for using Skipper: drive safely, the narration is AI-generated and may be wrong, credits aren't refundable, and there's no warranty."
  updated="August 6, 2026"
>
  <div class="legal-summary framed">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Drive first, listen second.</strong> Skipper is audio. Don't look at your phone.</li>
      <li>The Skipper is an AI narrator working from public sources. He is confident. He is not infallible.</li>
      <li>Credits are a license to generate drives. They aren't money, aren't refundable, and have no cash value.</li>
      <li>This is a small labor of love, provided as-is, with no warranty.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    These terms are between you and <strong>Manoa, Inc.</strong> ("we", "us"), the maker of Skipper. By
    using the app or this site, you're agreeing to them. If you don't, don't use Skipper. No hard
    feelings.
  </p>

  <h2>Drive safely. Seriously.</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Skipper is designed to be heard, not watched.</strong> That's the entire point of it: the
    Skipper talks so you can keep your eyes on the road and the lake instead of on a screen. Set up your
    drive before you put the car in gear.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Never operate the app while driving.</strong> Don't read the screen, don't tap the map,
      don't pick a stop. If you need to touch it, pull over somewhere safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skipper is not a navigation app.</strong> It doesn't give driving directions and it isn't
      built to be followed. Use a real navigation app, or a map, or your eyes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The road is the authority.</strong> Obey traffic laws, road signs, closures, and conditions
      always, and especially over anything Skipper says. If the Skipper cheerfully describes a viewpoint
      and there's a closure sign in front of it, the sign wins.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pull-offs, viewpoints, and stops are suggestions, not assessments.</strong> We haven't
      inspected them. Whether it's safe or legal to stop somewhere is your call, in the moment, with your
      own eyes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>You are responsible for your driving. Nothing in the app changes that.</p>

  <h2>About the narration</h2>
  <p>
    The Skipper's stories are <strong>AI-generated narration, grounded in public sources</strong>:
    mostly Wikipedia, plus Wikidata, Macrostrat, and Google Places. We work reasonably hard at accuracy:
    the stories are built from curated fact sheets rather than invented, every clip is checked against its
    sources before it ships, and a place with thin sources gets a scenic remark instead of a story,
    because silence beats a made-up battle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Even so: <strong>it can be wrong.</strong> Sources go stale, facts get contested, and an AI narrator
    can misread a good source with total confidence. Treat the Skipper the way you'd treat a charming
    uncle who has read a lot: enjoyable, frequently right, not a citation. Don't rely on it for anything
    that matters, and please don't repeat it as fact at a trivia night without checking.
  </p>
  <p>
    He's also a corny old tour guide by design. The jokes are jokes. Any opinion he offers is a bit, not
    a position of Manoa, Inc.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning a drive is a live conversation with an AI</h2>
  <p>
    The narration above is recorded in advance. <strong>Planning</strong> is not: you plan a drive by
    typing to the Skipper, and that half is a live AI model, run by <strong>Anthropic</strong> on our
    behalf. What you type in that conversation is sent to them to generate his reply. We don't keep
    the conversation. The <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> sets out exactly what travels with it
    and what doesn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things we'll ask of you there. <strong>Keep it about the drive</strong>: don't type anything
    into it you wouldn't hand to a third party: passwords, payment details, someone else's personal
    information, or anything you aren't free to share. And <strong>don't try to make him
    misbehave</strong>: no working the model for its instructions, no using the conversation as a
    general-purpose assistant, and no automating it.
  </p>
  <p>
    His replies are generated, so the accuracy caveat above applies here too, with one useful
    difference. He can only offer you places from a list we curated by hand, and that list is enforced
    on our side, not left to his good judgement. So he cannot send you somewhere that doesn't exist,
    and when he says he doesn't know a place, that is the honest answer rather than a failure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning is <strong>free and unmetered</strong>, and we mean to keep it that way; a credit is
    only ever spent when you tell him to build the drive. Because every message does cost us real
    money, we may <strong>rate-limit it, or suspend access</strong>, where it's being used abusively
    or automatically. If that ever catches you by mistake, email us and we'll sort it out.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can use Skipper</h2>
  <p>
    You need to be <strong>18 or older</strong> to make an account. If you're between 13 and 18, or
    under the age of majority where you live, you can use Skipper under the supervision of a parent or
    guardian who agrees to these terms on your behalf. Younger kids are extremely welcome in the back
    seat; the account just isn't theirs.
  </p>

  <h2>Your account</h2>
  <p>
    Planning a drive works without an account; making one needs it. You're responsible for what happens
    under your account, so keep your sign-in code, and your password if you set one, to yourself. Give us
    an email address that's actually yours and keep it current: we sign you in by emailing a code to it,
    so it isn't just how you get back in, it's how you get in at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can delete your account from Settings at any time. It's immediate and it's total; see the
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for exactly what gets erased.
  </p>

  <h2>Credits</h2>
  <p>
    Generating a drive costs a credit. New accounts get an allotment of free ones. Here's the honest
    shape of that:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A credit is a <strong>limited, personal, non-transferable license</strong> to have us generate one
      drive. It is not money, not a currency, not property, and not a stored-value balance.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credits have <strong>no cash value</strong>, can't be sold, traded, or given away, and can't be
      converted into anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credits are <strong>not refundable</strong>. A credit is spent when the drive is generated; that's
      the moment the work happens, and the work doesn't un-happen. Deleting the drive afterwards doesn't
      give it back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deleting your account forfeits any credits you have left.</strong> Erasure is total; the
      ledger goes with it. Spend them first if you care about them.
    </li>
    <li>
      We may change the free allotment, or how credits work, for future drives. We won't retroactively
      take away credits you already have.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you ever buy credits, anything you buy through the App Store is also subject to Apple's terms, and
    refunds for those purchases are handled by Apple under their policy.
  </p>

